---
title: Setting up specimen records
sidebar:
  label: Setup
  order: 1
---

The NAHPU default lists for `Specimen parts` and `Treatments` are intentionally
short. Before entering a large batch of specimens, configure the project lists
that your team will use in the field or collection room.

{% steps %}

1. Navigate to the dashboard.
2. Tap/click the hamburger menu in the top left corner.
3. Select `Settings`.
4. Under `Catalogs`, select `Specimens`.
5. Under `Field ID`, choose how new specimens are numbered:

    - `Personnel ID` (default): the Cataloger's initials and their own running
      number. Add a person with the `Cataloger` role to the project and give
      them a current field number.
    - `Project ID`: a `Prefix`, `Current catalog number`, and `Suffix`, joined
      directly. Turn on `Auto-increment catalog number` to advance the number
      each time a specimen uses it.

    Select `Save field ID settings` to apply the choice.

    This is only the default. Open a specimen's `Edit field ID` to choose
    Personnel or Project for that specimen. A project can contain both kinds,
    but one specimen cannot have both identifiers.

    {% aside type="caution" %}

    Changing a prefix or suffix changes how existing project IDs display, and
    NAHPU asks you to confirm. Those IDs may already be in exports or on
    specimen labels, so check the values first.

    {% /aside %}

6. Add `Tissue ID` (optional).

    Some museums assign separate tissue numbers for the same voucher specimen.
    You can set up a prefix, the next number, and a suffix here. Leading zeros
    in the number are kept, so `M0042-T` is followed by `M0043-T`. NAHPU can
    then generate the next tissue number when you add a specimen part.

    For the Ichthyology catalog format, `Individual tag ID` sets the same kind
    of numbering for individuals in a fish lot.

7. Add `Specimen types`.

    Add all preparation and tissue types you expect to use, including types you
    may only collect occasionally.

8. Add `Treatments`.

    Treatments are used on specimen parts. Examples include `None`, `ETOH`,
    `Formalin`, `LN2`, and `DMSO`.

{% /steps %}

The same screen also holds the lists for `Conditions`, `Identification Methods`,
and `Life Stages`, and `Always show collector field`. Review them before the
field season too.

{% aside type="tip" %}

Specimen types and treatments are user configs. Share them with
`Export user configs` and `Import user configs` so another NAHPU installation
uses the same controlled lists.

See [Settings](/en/usages/settings/) for the complete configuration map.

{% /aside %}
